## Why This Is Asked
Auditing across a CNPA stack is critical for compliance; beginners should show end-to-end thinking and minimal latency impact.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end provenance via a single audit_id
- Propagation through HTTP headers and Kafka message headers
- Lightweight audit table and minimal schema to avoid latency
- End-to-end tests that verify trace consistency

## Code Example
```javascript
// Generate and propagate audit_id in Node.js
function handleRequest(req, res) {
  const auditId = req.headers['x-audit-id'] || generateAuditId();
  res.setHeader('x-audit-id', auditId);
  // log audit locally
  logAudit({ auditId, endpoint: req.path, userId: req.user?.id, ts: Date.now() });
  // forward to DB and Kafka with the header
  db.insert({ audit_id: auditId, ...req.body });
  kafka.publish('events', { payload: req.body, headers: { 'x-audit-id': auditId } });
  res.end('ok');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test end-to-end audit trace under load? 
- What metrics would you monitor for audit latency and completeness?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests end-to-end reasoning across CNPA components, focusing on correctness under concurrent event processing and deployment scenarios.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end tracing
- Kafka producer ordering
- Consumer offset management
- Redis cache invalidation
- Event versioning and idempotent sinks
- Cache stampede protection

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example snippet: idempotent sink for Redis
async function sinkEvent(event) {
  const key = event.key;
  const ver = event.version;
  const current = await redis.get(key);
  if (!current || current.version < ver) {
    await redis.set(key, JSON.stringify(event));
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Assess ability to implement privacy-by-design in a CNPA pipeline, balancing latency, security, and analytics usability.

## Key Concepts

- Data masking and tokenization
- End-to-end privacy across streaming sinks
- Key management and rotation
- Data lineage and auditing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode: masks PII before Kafka publish
function maskPII(record, tenantSalt){
  if(!record) return record;
  record.email = hash(record.email + tenantSalt);
  record.phone = redactPhone(record.phone);
  return record;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you rotate masking keys without service downtime?
- How would you validate that analytics still yield correct aggregates with masked data?
